    "slug": "bilingual-spanish-english-seo-for-a-florida-medical-practice",
    "keyword": "bilingual Spanish and English SEO for a Florida medical practice",
    "category": "Playbook",
    "title": "Bilingual Spanish and English SEO for a Florida Medical Practice",
    "metaTitle": "Spanish and English SEO for a Florida Medical Practice",
    "metaDescription": "How a Florida medical practice can set up Spanish pages, hreflang and listings so Spanish-speaking patients find them. Call (561) 291-2681.",
    "answer": "Build real Spanish pages at their own web addresses, link each to its English twin with hreflang, translate titles and descriptions too, and have a fluent person review everything before it goes live. Research Spanish search terms separately. Only mention Spanish-language care if your staff or interpreters can truly provide it.",
    "author": "Gio LaRoche",
    "publishDate": "2026-10-06T09:00:00Z",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-06",
    "readMinutes": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Start with the patient, not the translation"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Offering a Spanish version of your site is a promise that a Spanish-speaking patient can get care in the language they read. Before you build anything, confirm who can actually deliver it: a Spanish-speaking provider, staff, or a qualified interpreter service. The website should match that reality, in the page copy, on your Google profile and at the front desk. A Spanish page that leads to an English-only phone tree frustrates the patient it was built for. Language-access duties for health care are a legal question for your counsel, and this guide covers only search."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Give each language its own page"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Google recommends a separate address for each language version, such as a /es/ folder, rather than swapping text on the same URL. Each page then has its own title, description and headings, and each can rank on its own. Also set the language in the page code so browsers and assistive technology read it correctly."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Element",
          "What to do in Spanish"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "URL",
            "A clear, readable address in the Spanish folder, such as /es/medicina-familiar/."
          ],
          [
            "Title and meta description",
            "Rewrite them in Spanish, with the terms patients use."
          ],
          [
            "Headings and body",
            "Translate fully and review for natural phrasing, not word-for-word."
          ],
          [
            "Image alt text",
            "Translate it, so a Spanish screen reader user gets the same description."
          ],
          [
            "Navigation and footer",
            "Translate the menu so the visitor stays in Spanish."
          ],
          [
            "Phone, address and hours",
            "Keep identical to the English page."
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Connect the two versions with hreflang"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Hreflang tags tell Google which page is the Spanish version of which English page. Every English page lists its Spanish twin, every Spanish page lists its English twin, and each also lists itself. The links must point both ways, or Google may ignore them. Add a visible language switcher too, and avoid redirecting visitors automatically by browser language, because it can trap people and hide a version from crawlers. Our [schema guide](/guides/schema-markup-for-a-medical-practice-website-explained) shows how structured data should match each language version."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Machine translation: use it carefully"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "A raw automatic translation published without review reads badly, and Google's guidance on localized pages cautions against it. Its spam policies also warn against auto-generated text published without human review. Use a tool for a first draft if you like, then have a fluent person who knows medical terms edit every page. Medical vocabulary differs between regions, and a mistranslated symptom or procedure is worse than none. Keep claims identical in both languages, and do not add clinical promises in one version that the other lacks. Plan on re-reviewing the Spanish copy whenever the English page changes, since a stale translation is a common way for the two versions to drift apart."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Spanish keyword research and listings"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Search terms are not simple translations. Patients may type médico familiar, clínica cerca de mí, or an English term inside a Spanish sentence. Check Google Search Console for Spanish queries you already get, and test searches yourself, as covered in our [keyword research guide](/guides/keyword-research-for-a-medical-practice-seo-campaign)."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "On your Google profile, the business description can include a sentence in Spanish. Some categories offer a languages-spoken attribute, which you should select only if it is true. See our guide to [profile attributes](/guides/google-business-profile-attributes-for-a-medical-practice). Keep one profile per location. Do not create a second Spanish profile, which would be a duplicate listing."
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Do I need separate Spanish pages for SEO?",
        "a": "Yes, if you want the Spanish version to rank. Google recommends a separate URL for each language so each page can have its own title, description and content. Overlaying text on the same address is harder for Google to understand and index."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is hreflang and do I need it?",
        "a": "Hreflang is a tag that tells Google which page is the equivalent in another language. It helps Google show the Spanish page to Spanish-language searchers and the English page to others. Use it on every page that has a translation, and make the links reciprocal."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can I use Google Translate to create my Spanish pages?",
        "a": "As a first draft, perhaps, but not as the finished page. Unreviewed machine translation reads poorly and can mistranslate medical terms. Google's guidance cautions against publishing it without human review. Have a fluent person who understands medical vocabulary edit every page before it goes live."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should I claim my practice offers Spanish-language care?",
        "a": "Only if it does. State it only when a provider, staff member or qualified interpreter can serve patients in Spanish. Claiming it falsely harms patients and your reputation. Match the website, the Google profile and the front desk to the same answer."
      },
      {
        "q": "Do I need a second Google Business Profile for Spanish?",
        "a": "No. Use one profile per location and add Spanish where the profile allows, such as in the description. A second profile for the same location is a duplicate listing, and duplicates can cause suspensions and leave patients unsure which listing is current."
      }
    ],
