    "slug": "how-to-publish-self-pay-prices-on-a-medical-practice-website",
    "keyword": "how to publish self-pay prices and fees on a medical practice website",
    "category": "How-to",
    "title": "How to Publish Self-Pay Prices on a Medical Practice Website",
    "metaTitle": "How to Publish Self-Pay Prices on a Practice Website",
    "metaDescription": "How a medical practice can list self-pay prices and fees on its website clearly and honestly, with page structure and cautions. Call (561) 291-2681.",
    "answer": "Publish self-pay prices in a plain table: the service, what is included, the price or a true starting price, what costs extra, and how to book. Date the page and keep it current. List only prices you will honor, never invent ranges, and ask your attorney which price rules apply to your practice.",
    "author": "Gio LaRoche",
    "publishDate": "2026-10-06T09:00:00Z",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-06",
    "readMinutes": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Why patients look for prices"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Patients searching for how much a visit, a procedure or a membership costs are often self-paying, uninsured, or comparing options. A page that answers the question gets read, and it saves your front desk phone calls that start with price. A page that hides the number sends the patient to the next result. Clear prices also set expectations before the first call, which tends to make the first conversation with the front desk shorter and calmer. This is useful for direct primary care, cash-pay clinics, med spas and any practice with self-pay visits, as in our [direct primary care playbook](/guides/direct-primary-care-marketing-playbook)."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What the law and the regulators say, and what they do not"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "We are a marketing agency and this is not legal advice. Two points are worth knowing. First, federal rules under the No Surprises Act give uninsured and self-pay patients a right to a good faith estimate of expected charges from providers when they schedule or ask, and CMS explains the details on its site. Second, the federal hospital price transparency rule applies to hospitals and is not a rule for a typical small practice, but rules and expectations change, and state rules vary. Ask your attorney what applies to you before you publish."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What a price page should contain"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Element",
          "Why it matters"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Service name",
            "Use the words patients search for."
          ],
          [
            "What is included",
            "Visit length, tests, supplies and follow-up included in the price."
          ],
          [
            "Price or true starting price",
            "Only a number you will charge. Say what moves it up."
          ],
          [
            "What costs extra",
            "Labs, imaging, medication, procedures or supplies billed separately."
          ],
          [
            "How to pay",
            "Cash, card, membership, payment plans, if you offer them."
          ],
          [
            "How to book",
            "A phone number and a booking link."
          ],
          [
            "Review date",
            "The date the prices were last confirmed."
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Do not invent prices to fill the table. If a service is quoted individually, say so and explain what the quote depends on and how to request one. A sentence such as the price depends on the visit type and any tests ordered is more honest than a guess, and it still answers the question patients came with."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Build it so it stays accurate"
      },
      {
        "type": "ol",
        "items": [
          "Put the prices on one page per service line, not scattered through blog posts.",
          "Use text in a table, not a picture of a price list, so Google and screen readers can read it.",
          "Add the last-reviewed date and a named person who owns updating it.",
          "Update the page the same day a price changes, and tell the front desk.",
          "Link to it from your service pages, contact page and our [insurance page guide](/guides/how-to-build-an-accepted-insurance-page-for-a-medical-practice)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "A \"starting at\" price is a claim. It must be a price some patients actually pay, and the page must say what else may apply. The FTC's advertising guidance asks that claims be truthful and not misleading."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Keep claims about results out of the page"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Prices are facts. Pair them with plain descriptions of the service, not promises about outcomes or comparisons with other practices. For ad copy that mentions price, check our [healthcare ad compliance checklist](/guides/healthcare-ad-compliance-checklist). Never place a patient's own bill, name or details on the page, even as an example. Build any worked example from a made-up visit that no real person could be matched to, and label it as an illustration."
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Do I have to publish my prices?",
        "a": "There is no single rule for every small practice. Federal law requires good faith estimates for uninsured and self-pay patients on request or when scheduling, and hospitals have separate transparency rules. Whether to publish is a business choice. Ask your attorney what applies to you."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is it okay to say starting at on a price page?",
        "a": "Yes, if it is true. The starting price should be one some patients actually pay, and the page should say what can raise it. A starting figure that almost nobody gets is misleading and risks a complaint under advertising rules."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is a good faith estimate?",
        "a": "A good faith estimate is a written estimate of expected charges for items and services that providers must give uninsured or self-pay patients under the No Surprises Act. The patient can request one, and CMS describes the rules on its consumer pages."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should prices be in a table or in a PDF?",
        "a": "Use a table in the page itself. Text in a table can be read by search engines, voice assistants and screen readers, while a PDF or an image is harder to use and update. Include the date you last confirmed the prices."
      },
      {
        "q": "How often should self-pay prices be updated?",
        "a": "Whenever a price changes, and reviewed at least twice a year, even when nothing changed. A stale price page causes complaints and front-desk disputes. Put a last-reviewed date on the page and name a person responsible for it, so updating it never depends on anyone's memory."
      }
    ],
