    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "The three types in plain words"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Google sorts profiles by where service happens. A storefront serves customers at its address during staffed hours. A service-area business goes to its customers, so it lists the areas it covers instead of, or in addition to, an address. A hybrid does both: customers visit the location and staff also travel. These are Google's own categories, and its guidelines for representing your business on Google decide which one applies to you. A profile that is set up correctly can still be missing from results, and [why a practice isn't showing up on Google Maps](/blog/why-your-medical-practice-isnt-showing-up-on-google-maps) walks through the usual reasons."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Type",
          "Typical medical example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Storefront",
            "A family practice, dental office or urgent care where patients come in."
          ],
          [
            "Service-area",
            "A house-call physician, mobile phlebotomy or in-home care provider with no patient office."
          ],
          [
            "Hybrid",
            "An office that also sends clinicians on home visits."
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "How to decide"
      },
      {
        "type": "ol",
        "items": [
          "Do patients visit a staffed location during posted hours? If yes, you are a storefront or hybrid, and the address must be shown.",
          "Do your clinicians go to patients at home and you have no place patients visit? Then you are a service-area business.",
          "Do you do both? Choose hybrid, show the address and list your service areas.",
          "Is the address a mailbox, a virtual office or a shared space nobody staffs? That address is not eligible, whatever type you choose.",
          "Do you only offer telehealth with no in-person location? Read Google's guidelines carefully, since a business without an in-person presence may not qualify for a profile at all."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What Google asks of each type"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "A storefront shows a public address and staffed hours, and the address must match your signage and website. A service-area business hides its address from the profile, lists the areas it covers within the limits Google sets, and still needs a real base, which Google may ask about during verification. Google limits how many areas you can list and how far from your base, so confirm the current limits on its service-area help page. Never list areas just to appear in more places, since that is the common route to a suspension. Our guide on [fixing a suspended profile](/guides/fix-a-suspended-google-business-profile) covers what happens next."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What changes for patients and for search"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "A hidden address means no pin on the map, and patients cannot see where to go, which suits house calls and confuses office visits. Hiding the address of a practice that sees patients in person violates the guidelines and misleads patients. Patients of a house-call practice, by contrast, only need the phone number, the covered areas and the hours. Local results depend on distance, relevance and prominence, and Google makes no promise about placement for any type. For a hidden-address profile, rank tracking tools that rely on the map pin do not work well, so see our guide on [tracking local rankings](/guides/how-to-track-local-search-rankings-for-a-medical-practice) for what to use."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Switching and checking"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "If you chose wrong, edit the profile rather than creating another one, since duplicates cause trouble. After any change, confirm the address, hours and service areas on the live profile, and make sure your website and directories match. Look at the profile in a private browser window, since that is what a patient sees, and screenshot it for your records. Verification may be needed again, as our guide on [verifying a profile](/guides/how-to-verify-a-google-business-profile-for-a-medical-practice) explains. The type also shapes your [category choices](/guides/choosing-google-business-profile-categories-for-a-medical-practice)."
      }
    ],
